WEBVTT

00:00:00.637 --> 00:00:18.805
<v Casey>Hey there — today we've got a rattlesnake that carries its own antidote in its blood, ten times more potent than current antivenom, and a study out of ETH Zurich arguing Earth's water didn't come from where the textbooks say. Plus the question underneath both: who actually decides what gets carried forward. Coming right up.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:51.440
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Sunday, September twentieth, twenty twenty-six. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations. Today's theme is Science, Wonder, and the Natural World.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:01:07.160
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We've got a rattlesnake that carries its own antidote in its blood, a philosophy fight about whether your notebook counts as part of your brain, and a paper arguing Earth's water didn't come from where textbooks say it did. Good day for this one.

00:01:07.843 --> 00:01:30.171
<v Casey>Quick weather check before we get into it. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning, mainly clear, around 10 degrees, high of 24 today, clouding over through the afternoon, no rain expected. Across the region — 100 Mile House around 10 and mainly clear, Williams Lake around 8 and partly cloudy, Quesnel around 10 with overcast skies, Tatla Lake around 8 and partly cloudy.

00:01:30.515 --> 00:01:33.915
<v Riley>A good stretch to be outside before that cloud rolls in. Let's get into it.

00:01:35.315 --> 00:01:46.491
<v Riley>Five stories close to home today, then a science file that runs ten pieces deep. Nominations close October first for the twenty-ninth annual Williams Lake Business Excellence Awards.

00:01:47.896 --> 00:01:54.152
<v Casey>Twenty-nine years running is its own kind of durability — most awards programs don't survive one recession, let alone this many.

00:01:55.475 --> 00:02:12.123
<v Riley>Anyone can submit a nomination, and the deadline gives businesses about ten days from today to get their paperwork in. Cariboo-Chilcotin MLA Lorne Doerkson presented at the 2024 ceremony, so it carries some political weight alongside the local business recognition.

00:02:13.323 --> 00:02:22.483
<v Casey>Provincial politics had a chaotic week. Eight former B.C. Conservative MLAs have broken off to form a new party, CentreBC, with Peter Milobar named leader as of Friday.

00:02:22.383 --> 00:02:32.079
<v Riley>Same day, the B.C. Conservatives removed three MLAs from caucus — Trevor Halford, Claire Rattée, and John Rustad — over the leadership fight with Kerry-Lynne Findlay.

00:02:33.271 --> 00:02:41.919
<v Casey>Rattée told reporters she's "not going anywhere," which tells you this isn't a quiet resignation. She's one of ten MLAs on record calling for Findlay to step down.

00:02:43.357 --> 00:02:56.493
<v Riley>So you've got a party that's lost eight members to a breakaway and expelled three more from what's left, in the same week. That's a caucus in open revolt, a little over a year out from the next provincial election.

00:02:57.692 --> 00:03:12.236
<v Casey>For a riding like Cariboo-Chilcotin, that kind of instability makes it harder for any one MP or MLA to promise continuity on regional files — forestry transition, highway funding, health staffing — because nobody knows which party banner they'll be sitting under by spring.

00:03:13.435 --> 00:03:26.779
<v Riley>Closer to home. A bear that had been hanging around 100 Mile House for several days was tranquilized and euthanized by the BC Conservation Officer Service. The Tribune published a letter about it this weekend.

00:03:28.245 --> 00:03:40.085
<v Casey>The letter writer's argument is the one you hear every September — unsecured garbage and unpicked fruit trees draw bears into town, and by the time COS gets the call, relocation usually isn't realistic anymore.

00:03:41.412 --> 00:03:51.852
<v Riley>And it's a decent setup for what the Conservation Society's doing in the spotlight later — most of this is preventable with better attractant management, not more relocations.

00:03:53.052 --> 00:04:07.164
<v Riley>On a lighter note — a Williams Lake candle maker is opening her first storefront. Lana Nunn runs Rustic Country Candles, and after years of building the business online and at farmers markets, she's signed a lease for a physical space downtown.

00:04:08.552 --> 00:04:16.976
<v Casey>That's the kind of story that never gets a press release or a ribbon-cutting photo op — just someone who did the math on foot traffic and signed the paperwork herself.

00:04:18.176 --> 00:04:36.112
<v Riley>Now the science file, and it opens with a real reversal. ScienceDaily reported this week, out of ETH Zurich, that Earth may have formed almost entirely from material already present in the inner solar system — not water-rich debris flung in from beyond Jupiter, which has been the standard model for decades.

00:04:37.479 --> 00:04:45.423
<v Casey>If that holds up, the water in this room, in every lake in the Cariboo, arrived here far earlier and from a lot closer than the textbook story says.

00:04:46.754 --> 00:04:55.858
<v Riley>The researchers used the word "astonished," and for once it's not a headline writer overselling a modest result. This reworks the origin story of our own water.

00:04:57.041 --> 00:05:06.521
<v Casey>Astonished is still a word a press office chose. The underlying claim earns it — a study that could rewrite a chapter that's been settled since before either of us existed.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:25.336
<v Riley>Staying with big discoveries but going underground instead of into space — Ivanhoe's Makoko copper find in the Democratic Republic of Congo just got thirty percent bigger after another round of drilling. It's moving toward a formal mine-planning phase with a 2027 scoping study targeted.

00:05:26.627 --> 00:05:41.931
<v Casey>Copper is the metal every grid-modernization story and every EV story quietly depends on. Whoever controls a find that size controls a piece of the supply chain that nobody in a town like Quesnel gets a vote on, even as the price eventually shows up in a transformer upgrade here.

00:05:43.131 --> 00:05:57.347
<v Riley>There's also a new study getting attention this week — people who describe themselves as having "nothing to hide" turn out to be statistically more likely to snoop on other people's private information, according to research covered by ZDNet.

00:05:58.547 --> 00:06:12.667
<v Casey>New York City released more than a hundred and seventy thousand pages of documents this week showing officials repeatedly misled residents about air quality near the World Trade Center site in the months after September eleventh, two thousand and one.

00:06:14.078 --> 00:06:24.238
<v Riley>Twenty-five years later, and the official record only now says plainly what a lot of first responders and downtown residents already knew from getting sick years ahead of schedule.

00:06:25.438 --> 00:06:35.886
<v Casey>Meanwhile fuel prices are setting off protests worldwide — Indonesia, Guatemala, Syria — as oil pushes past a hundred dollars a barrel, largely tied to the ongoing conflict involving Iran.

00:06:37.312 --> 00:06:46.688
<v Riley>Rural British Columbia runs on trucks, generators, and heating oil. A hundred-dollar barrel doesn't stay an overseas headline for long once it hits the pump in Williams Lake.

00:06:47.888 --> 00:07:03.632
<v Casey>On the human-body side of the science file, NPR reported new evidence of ancient betel nut use — researchers examining human remains found signs the habit is far older than assumed, tied to a mind-altering seed still chewed across parts of Asia today despite well-documented health risks.

00:07:04.832 --> 00:07:16.416
<v Riley>The Verge published a piece this week on cars quietly collecting and selling driver data — location history, driving habits, in some newer models even biometric information from the seat sensors.

00:07:17.615 --> 00:07:27.863
<v Casey>Country Life ran a long piece on the Norman Conquest — dovecotes, deer parks, vineyards, castles — arguing the Normans reshaped the English landscape as thoroughly as they reshaped its politics.

00:07:28.976 --> 00:07:37.096
<v Riley>A thousand years later, and you can still trace land-use patterns back to decisions one invading court made about what counted as worth building.

00:07:38.275 --> 00:07:43.579
<v Casey>Someone was deciding what got preserved even then, long before anyone called it heritage policy.

00:07:44.779 --> 00:08:04.467
<v Riley>The same magazine profiled art conservators working to restore old paintings — pigment chemistry, infrared imaging, and years of training brought to bear on canvases centuries old. That's science put directly in service of deciding what gets to survive another hundred years. We'll come back to that idea in a few minutes.

00:08:05.667 --> 00:08:19.651
<v Casey>Last one in the file — EarthSky is tracking whether fall migration produces what birders call a "billion bird night" this year, and reminding people that switching off exterior lights overnight helps migrating birds navigate safely past built-up areas.

00:08:21.014 --> 00:08:26.206
<v Riley>Something anyone in the Cariboo can do tonight without spending a dollar or waiting on a grant.

00:08:27.106 --> 00:08:45.242
<v Casey>Before we move to the spotlight — a correction. In a recent episode, we said fifteen candidates had filed for Williams Lake council. A listener, Thomas Schoen, wrote in to tell us one candidate withdrew his name three days before that episode aired, which puts the actual number at fourteen. Thanks for catching that, Thomas.

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:51.066
<v Riley>Good thing to get right before ballots go to print. Let's move to today's spotlight.

00:08:52.465 --> 00:09:34.769
<v Riley>Today's spotlight is on the Cariboo Chilcotin Conservation Society — a local nonprofit that's been running environmental education programs across the region since 1996. Water Wise, Waste Wise, Skates for All, and school field programs that get kids out on Scout Island and other local sites learning how the ecosystems around them actually function. It happens to land on National Day of the Hunter, and the Society's work is a good reminder that ethical hunting has always been woven into the Cariboo's conservation story rather than sitting apart from it. Knowing a landscape well enough to hunt it responsibly and knowing it well enough to protect it tend to come from the same close attention over years.

00:09:35.294 --> 00:09:43.766
<v Casey>Worth a look at conservationsociety.ca, especially if you've got a kid who asks too many questions about where the water in the tap actually comes from.

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:46.720
<v Riley>Which is a decent bridge into today's Deep Dive.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:10:21.696
<v Riley>Rattlesnakes carry their own antidote. That's the detail that stopped me cold. Researchers published in PNAS this month that rattlesnake blood contains a family of proteins — FETUA-family metalloproteinase inhibitors, if you want the technical name — that neutralize venom from several dangerous viper species. They identified five members of that protein family in the genus Crotalus, more than were previously known to exist. In lab mixtures, combinations of these proteins were about ten times more potent against venom than a current commercial antivenom.

00:10:22.328 --> 00:10:28.656
<v Casey>The predator carrying the cure to its own weapon in its own bloodstream. Good story before you even get to the medicine.

00:10:29.259 --> 00:10:45.315
<v Riley>It's evolution solving a problem nobody asked it to solve for us. The snake didn't develop this so humans could survive snakebite — it's a byproduct of whatever selective pressure built the snake's own resistance to its neighbors' venom. Now researchers get to borrow the solution.

00:10:45.967 --> 00:11:04.783
<v Casey>Lab tests, though. In vitro. Not a treatment anyone's receiving in an emergency room yet. Antivenom development has historically taken years, sometimes closer to a decade, to move from a bench discovery to something a regulator will approve for human injection — dosing has to be worked out, manufacturing has to scale, safety trials have to run.

00:11:05.438 --> 00:11:13.494
<v Riley>The discovery itself is real, and it's not a marginal improvement. Ten times more potent gets pharmaceutical attention on its own.

00:11:13.994 --> 00:11:39.282
<v Casey>Snakebite is a World Health Organization-listed neglected tropical disease. Hundreds of thousands of people are affected every year, mostly in low-resource regions across South Asia and sub-Saharan Africa. In most of those places, the bottleneck was never potency — it's access, whether any antivenom at all is stocked at the nearest clinic, whether that clinic is a two-hour walk or a two-day walk away, whether the patient can afford it once they arrive.

00:11:39.897 --> 00:11:44.929
<v Riley>That's a manufacturing and distribution problem. Doesn't make the science less real.

00:11:45.589 --> 00:12:06.757
<v Casey>They're the same problem once you ask who decides the ten-times-more-potent version is worth building at scale. A pharmaceutical company weighing return on a Phase 3 trial. A government health ministry setting procurement priorities. A research funder choosing which papers turn into products. It's rarely the person who actually gets bitten by a saw-scaled viper in rural Bihar.

00:12:07.357 --> 00:12:14.085
<v Riley>So the elegant nature story and the ugly access story turn out to be the same molecule, viewed from two different rooms.

00:12:14.694 --> 00:12:25.486
<v Casey>The snake already solved its half of the problem for free, millions of years ago. Humans still have to decide whose life that solution reaches first, and that decision happens in a boardroom, not a lab.

00:12:25.886 --> 00:12:36.294
<v Riley>Which gets close to something worth asking straight out today. Who's actually in the room when the call gets made about what's worth carrying forward and what gets left on the bench.

00:12:36.394 --> 00:12:54.002
<v Riley>With the antivenom it's a funding committee and a regulatory pathway years down the line. With a species-at-risk listing it's a government scientific panel that never meets the species in question. Either way, the decision happens somewhere the person who actually needs the outcome doesn't get a seat.

00:12:54.563 --> 00:13:09.387
<v Casey>And usually well after the interesting science is already done and published. The hard part was never discovering the mechanism. It's the second decision — who builds it, who pays to scale it, who it gets built for first. Nobody wrote a press release about that part.

00:13:10.087 --> 00:13:29.735
<v Riley>There's a stranger version of the same question buried in a piece making the rounds about the extended mind thesis — the idea that when you write something down, or use a map, or work through a problem with a tool in your hands, the thinking isn't confined to your skull. It's happening in the loop between you and the object.

00:13:30.376 --> 00:13:52.304
<v Casey>Andy Clark and David Chalmers' original argument, from the nineties. It's a real position in philosophy of mind, but it's contested, not settled. Critics named Adams and Aizawa have spent years arguing that a notebook supports cognition — it doesn't become part of your cognition. There's a difference between a tool being coupled to your thinking and a tool actually extending what your mind is.

00:13:52.849 --> 00:14:09.153
<v Riley>Drawing that line too sharply undersells what's happening, though. If a hunter reads a game trail, or a hydrologist reads a stream gauge, the reading is doing something a bare brain sitting in a dark room couldn't do alone. Some of the thinking is happening out there, in the terrain itself.

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:31.388
<v Casey>Or the terrain is just handing your brain better data, which your brain then processes the ordinary way it processes everything else. That's the whole argument, not hairsplitting. Even Chalmers has had to go back and clarify what counts as genuine extended cognition versus something that only looks like it from the outside, because the first version of the claim didn't hold up to scrutiny.

00:14:31.974 --> 00:14:41.478
<v Riley>Even the skeptics agree tools change what's thinkable. Nobody disputes that a notebook lets you hold ten ideas at once that short-term memory alone couldn't carry.

00:14:42.005 --> 00:15:06.197
<v Casey>Nobody disputes usefulness. What's unsettled is whether "useful" earns the word "mind." And that's not just an academic argument over definitions — it's the same gatekeeping question as the antivenom, wearing a different coat. A philosopher decides what counts as cognition. A funding panel decides what counts as a treatable disease. A heritage board decides what counts as a building worth saving instead of a demolition permit.

00:15:06.697 --> 00:15:09.601
<v Riley>Huh. Hadn't put those three next to each other before.

00:15:10.167 --> 00:15:22.919
<v Casey>Same shape of decision every time you look closely. Somebody draws a line, and everything sitting on the wrong side of it doesn't get resources, doesn't get a species listing, doesn't get a research grant, doesn't get called a mind.

00:15:23.542 --> 00:15:44.150
<v Riley>Then the honest read on the natural-world side of this is that wonder alone doesn't get anything past the line. The rattlesnake story is remarkable on its own terms, and it still needs a regulatory pathway, a manufacturer willing to take the financial risk, and a distribution network reaching a clinic in a country that may not have reliable refrigeration.

00:15:44.834 --> 00:16:07.298
<v Casey>Wonder can even work against the thing that needs the resources most. A dramatic, photogenic discovery gets funded and covered ahead of a duller, more urgent one sitting one folder over. Nobody writes a breathless headline about routine antivenom stockpiling logistics in a rural clinic, even though that's the intervention that saves lives this year, not in five once a next-generation drug clears trials.

00:16:07.898 --> 00:16:29.754
<v Riley>There's a smaller version of exactly this sitting in today's letters section. The 100 Mile bear that got tranquilized and put down — that's the same triage question at a scale we can actually see. A conservation officer makes a call in the field about which animal gets relocated and which one doesn't, and most residents never see the criteria behind that decision.

00:16:30.393 --> 00:16:52.625
<v Casey>That's a real gap. The Conservation Society runs the prevention side — teaching people to secure garbage and pick fruit before a bear ever becomes habituated, so the call never has to be made in the first place. But the actual protocol for what happens once a bear is already in town sits with the province, and local input into that specific decision isn't something either of us can confirm exists.

00:16:53.204 --> 00:17:00.964
<v Riley>Is there a version of that decision that pulls in local knowledge before the animal's already standing at somebody's back door?

00:17:01.308 --> 00:17:21.412
<v Casey>Some regions run wildlife-conflict coordinators, under programs like WildSafeBC, who as publicly reported log conflict patterns and feed that information back to conservation officers. Whether that shapes the relocate-or-destroy decision itself, or just documents the pattern for next year's report, isn't something a letter to the editor settles either way.

00:17:21.981 --> 00:17:36.285
<v Riley>Still a concrete question worth sending along — whether the Cariboo Regional District, or a group like the Conservation Society, has any formal seat in that protocol, or whether it's purely a provincial call the moment an officer arrives on scene.

00:17:36.857 --> 00:17:51.529
<v Casey>Better question for WildSafeBC than a guess on air. We're working from published articles here, not a phone call with COS staff — a smaller confession than the one about a hundred-and-seventy-thousand pages of misleading air quality reports, but the same principle.

00:17:52.028 --> 00:18:09.004
<v Riley>So the pattern holds across every scale we've touched today. The interesting science happens first — the mechanism, the field data, the lab result. The decision about who that science actually serves happens later, somewhere quieter, usually without the person most affected in the room.

00:18:09.673 --> 00:18:19.233
<v Casey>And nobody put "who decides" on the agenda in the first place. It happens by default, and by default it usually goes to whoever already had a seat before the meeting started.

00:18:19.828 --> 00:18:31.492
<v Riley>I'd still call the rattlesnake finding a real win on its own terms. Even an unequal distribution system ends up better off with a ten-times-more-potent molecule inside it than without one.

00:18:32.124 --> 00:18:45.236
<v Casey>I'm not arguing the lab result is worthless. I'm just not calling it the finish line when the actual finish line is a kid in a rural clinic somewhere getting treated in time, and only one of those two things made it into the journal this month.

00:18:45.936 --> 00:19:00.304
<v Riley>Fair enough to end on that gap rather than paper over it — and if you've got thoughts on any of it, or actually know how the local wildlife-conflict decision works, who's consulted and who isn't, send us an email. Feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:19:00.979 --> 00:19:08.051
<v Casey>We'd like to know who's on that call list before the next bear shows up in someone's yard. That's my Sunday assignment, apparently.

00:19:08.344 --> 00:19:09.776
<v Riley>Mine too, then. Talk soon.